<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Isny</h1><p class="st-meta">1365 CE – 1803 CE · capital Isny im Allgäu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Allgäu linen town that bought its freedom in 1365 and turned Protestant while its abbey stayed Catholic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isny-im-allgau">Isny</a> grew around a Benedictine abbey founded in 1096 by the counts of Veringen-Altshausen. The market beside the abbey became a town in the 13th century and passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-waldburg">Truchsesses of Waldburg</a>, from whom it bought its freedom in 1365, becoming a free imperial city.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isny-im-allgau">Isny</a>'s prosperity came from linen weaving and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The town adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the 16th century; around 1540 the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-fagius">Paul Fagius</a> ran a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press there with the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah-levita">Elijah Levita</a>. The Benedictine abbey, which gained its own imperial status in 1781, remained a Catholic enclave.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 the town and abbey were given to the counts of Quadt as compensation for lost lands, and in 1806 they passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isny-im-allgau">Isny im Allgäu</a> keeps much of its wall and towers.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-isny.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Isny" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Wikiuser100 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cafe_in_Isny_im_Allgau,_Baden-Wurttemberg,_Germany.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1365 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Isny im Allgäu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Isny im Allgäu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1584&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-isny" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Isny on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4914 507 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Isny lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5234 763L5229 694M5234 763L5212 730"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5234 763h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5234 763h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Isny im Allgäu. <a href="/atlas?year=1584&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-isny">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
